## 3. Apps Script to Auto-Mark SUPERSEDED

Open **Extensions > Apps Script**, paste this, and save. Run `markSuperseded()` from the editor or attach it to a menu/button.

```javascript
function markSuperseded() {
  const ss    = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet();
  const sheet = ss.getSheetByName("Clean");
  const last  = sheet.getLastRow();
  if (last < 2) return;

  // Column indices (1-based): C = Email, I = Parsed Timestamp, O = Superseded flag
  const COL_EMAIL     = 3;
  const COL_TIMESTAMP = 9;
  const COL_FLAG      = 15;  // column O

  const data = sheet.getRange(2, 1, last - 1, sheet.getLastColumn()).getValues();

  // Group rows by lowercase trimmed email
  const groups = {};
  data.forEach((row, i) => {
    const email = String(row[COL_EMAIL - 1]).trim().toLowerCase();
    if (!email) return;                       // skip blank emails
    if (!groups[email]) groups[email] = [];
    groups[email].push({ idx: i, ts: row[COL_TIMESTAMP - 1] });
  });

  // Build output array for column O (preserve existing values)
  const flags = data.map(row => [row[COL_FLAG - 1] || ""]);

  Object.values(groups).forEach(entries => {
    if (entries.length < 2) return;           // no duplicates

    // Find the latest timestamp in the group
    let latest = entries[0];
    entries.forEach(e => {
      if (e.ts instanceof Date && latest.ts instanceof Date) {
        if (e.ts.getTime() > latest.ts.getTime()) latest = e;
      } else if (Number(e.ts) > Number(latest.ts)) {
        latest = e;
      }
    });

    // Mark every other entry as SUPERSEDED
    entries.forEach(e => {
      if (e.idx !== latest.idx) flags[e.idx] = ["SUPERSEDED"];
      else                      flags[e.idx] = [""];
    });
  });

  sheet.getRange(2, COL_FLAG, flags.length, 1).setValues(flags);
  SpreadsheetApp.flush();
  SpreadsheetApp.getUi().alert(
    "Done — marked " +
    flags.filter(f => f[0] === "SUPERSEDED").length +
    " rows as SUPERSEDED."
  );
}
```

A few things to note about the script. It only touches column O, so your other helper columns stay intact. It compares by email only; rows with no email are left alone for manual review. If you want to extend it to also dedupe by name when email is blank, let me know and I'll add that logic. You can re-run it safely — it recalculates every time rather than appending flags.

After running the script, refresh your Summary tab and the counts will update automatically since they're all formula-driven. Want me to add a chart setup or a "ready to export" filtered view next?

